## Learn: Glossary

- [Crypto Glossary](https://trustwallet.com/glossary): Definitions of essential Web3 and blockchain terms — written for both beginners and experienced users.

### Blockchain Fundamentals

- [What is Web3](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/web3): Web3 is the next generation of the internet, built on blockchain — decentralized, user-owned, and permissionless.
- [What is an Altcoin](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/altcoin): An altcoin is any cryptocurrency other than Bitcoin. Examples include Ethereum, BNB, Solana, and thousands more.
- [What is Bitcoin](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/bitcoin): Bitcoin (BTC) is the first and largest cryptocurrency by market cap, created in 2009 by Satoshi Nakamoto as decentralized digital money.
- [What is a Blockchain](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/blockchain): A blockchain is a distributed ledger where records (blocks) are linked cryptographically and stored across a network of nodes.
- [What is a Block](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/block): A block is a container of validated transactions that gets added to a blockchain at regular intervals, cryptographically linked to the previous block.
- [What is Cryptocurrency](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/cryptocurrency): Cryptocurrency is a digital asset secured by cryptography and recorded on a blockchain, enabling peer-to-peer transactions without banks.
- [What is Ethereum](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/ethereum): Ethereum (ETH) is the largest smart contract platform, powering DeFi, NFTs, and thousands of decentralized applications.
- [What is a Token](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/token): A token is a digital asset issued on an existing blockchain that represents value, utility, ownership, or access rights — distinct from a native coin which has its own underlying blockchain.
- [What is an Airdrop](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/airdrop): A crypto airdrop is the free distribution of tokens to wallet addresses, used by projects to bootstrap users, reward early adopters, or decentralize ownership.
- [What is the Halving](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/halving): The Bitcoin halving is a protocol event occurring every 210,000 blocks (~4 years) that cuts the block reward miners receive in half, reducing the rate at which new BTC enters circulation.
- [What is Mining](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/mining): Crypto mining is the process by which new coins are created and transactions are verified by solving complex computational puzzles, securing the blockchain through Proof of Work consensus.
- [What is Proof of Work](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/proof-of-work): Proof of Work (PoW) is a blockchain consensus mechanism where miners compete to solve computational puzzles to add new blocks, securing networks like Bitcoin through energy expenditure.
- [What is Proof of Stake](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/proof-of-stake): Proof of Stake (PoS) is a blockchain consensus mechanism that selects validators based on the amount of cryptocurrency they stake as collateral, offering an energy-efficient alternative to Proof of Work.
- [What is a Consensus Mechanism](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/consensus-mechanism): A consensus mechanism is the protocol used by a blockchain to agree on the valid state of the network across all distributed nodes, with Proof of Work and Proof of Stake being the two most widely used.
- [What is a Hash](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/hash): A cryptographic hash is a fixed-length output produced by running data through a one-way mathematical function, used in blockchain to identify blocks, verify transactions, and secure proof of work mining.
- [What is a Node](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/node): A node is any computer running blockchain software that stores a copy of the ledger, validates transactions, and helps maintain the decentralized network — forming the backbone of any cryptocurrency.
- [What is a Fork](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/fork): A blockchain fork is a divergence in a blockchain's protocol that creates two separate paths — either temporarily (soft fork) or permanently (hard fork) — often resulting in two distinct cryptocurrencies.
- [What is a Mempool](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/mempool): A mempool (memory pool) is a node's holding area for pending unconfirmed transactions waiting to be included in the next block, where transactions compete for inclusion based on fees.
- [What are Gas Fees](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/gas-fees): Gas fees are payments to blockchain validators for processing transactions. On Ethereum, fees are paid in ETH and vary based on network demand.
- [What is Layer 1](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/layer-1): A Layer 1 blockchain is a base blockchain protocol like Bitcoin, Ethereum, or Solana — the underlying network that provides security, consensus, and finality without depending on another chain.
- [What is Layer 2](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/layer-2): A Layer 2 is a secondary protocol built on top of a Layer 1 blockchain to improve scalability and reduce fees — examples include Arbitrum, Optimism, Base, and zkSync on Ethereum.
- [What is a Rollup](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/rollup): A rollup is a Layer 2 scaling solution that bundles many transactions off-chain and posts compressed proof back to the Layer 1, dramatically reducing gas fees.
- [What is a ZK Proof](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/zk-proof): A zero-knowledge proof (ZK proof) is a cryptographic method that proves the validity of a statement without revealing the underlying data, powering privacy-preserving rollups and identity systems.
- [What is a Bridge](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/bridge): A crypto bridge is a protocol that connects two blockchains, allowing assets to move between them. Bridges power cross-chain swaps, multi-chain DeFi, and asset portability.
- [What is a Smart Contract](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/smart-contract): A smart contract is self-executing code on a blockchain that automatically enforces agreement terms when conditions are met — no intermediaries needed.
- [What is ERC-20](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/erc-20): ERC-20 is the technical standard for fungible tokens on the Ethereum blockchain, defining a common set of rules so that tokens work consistently across wallets, exchanges, and apps. It's the most widely used token standard in crypto.
- [What is ERC-721](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/erc-721): ERC-721 is the Ethereum standard for non-fungible tokens (NFTs) — unique, indivisible tokens where each one is distinct from every other, used to represent digital art, collectibles, and ownership of one-of-a-kind items.
- [What is BEP-20](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/bep-20): BEP-20 is the token standard for fungible tokens on BNB Smart Chain (BSC), modeled on Ethereum's ERC-20 but with lower fees and faster transactions. It defines how tokens behave across BNB Chain wallets and apps.
- [What is TRC-20](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/trc-20): TRC-20 is the token standard for fungible tokens on the Tron blockchain, widely used for stablecoins like USDT thanks to its low fees and fast transactions — especially popular in emerging markets for transfers and remittances.
- [What is a Stablecoin](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/stablecoin): A stablecoin is a cryptocurrency designed to hold a steady value by being pegged to a stable asset — usually a fiat currency like the US dollar — combining the stability of traditional money with the speed and flexibility of crypto.
- [What is Cryptography](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/cryptography): Cryptography is the mathematics of securing information, and the foundation of crypto: it generates key pairs, signs transactions, and links blocks with hashes so ownership can be proven and history can't be quietly rewritten.
- [What is the EVM](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/evm): The Ethereum Virtual Machine (EVM) is the runtime that executes smart contracts on Ethereum — and because EVM-compatible chains run the same bytecode, apps, tokens, and wallets built for one work across all of them.
- [What is a Genesis Block](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/genesis-block): A genesis block is the first block ever recorded on a blockchain — block zero, hardcoded into the protocol, with no previous block to reference — and every later block traces its history back to it.
- [What is Hash Rate](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/hash-rate): Hash rate is the total computing power miners devote to a proof-of-work blockchain, measured in hashes per second — the higher it is, the harder and more expensive the network is to attack.
- [What is Interoperability](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/interoperability): Interoperability is the ability of different blockchains to exchange assets and data with each other — through bridges, messaging protocols, and shared standards — instead of operating as isolated islands.
- [What is a Sidechain](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/sidechain): A sidechain is an independent blockchain that runs alongside a main chain and connects to it through a two-way bridge, letting assets move between the two while the sidechain keeps its own rules, consensus, and security.
- [What is Chain Abstraction](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/chain-abstraction): Chain abstraction is the design goal of letting people use blockchain apps without thinking about which chain they're on — one balance and one interface, with bridging, routing, and gas handled behind the scenes.
- [What is x402](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/x402): x402 is an open payment protocol built on the HTTP 402 "Payment Required" status code that lets websites and APIs charge crypto — typically stablecoins — per request, so humans and AI agents can pay for resources instantly.
- [What is an Intent](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/intents): In crypto, an intent is a signed statement of the outcome you want — trade this for at least that — which specialized actors called solvers compete to execute on your behalf, instead of you crafting the exact transaction yourself.

### Wallets & Security

- [What is a Seed Phrase](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/seed-phrase): A seed phrase (recovery phrase) is a 12 or 24-word backup that controls access to a self-custody wallet. Never share it with anyone.
- [What is a Private Key](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/private-key): A private key is a secret cryptographic code that gives you full control of your cryptocurrency. Anyone with access to it controls your funds — never share it.
- [What is a Public Key](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/public-key): A public key is your shareable blockchain identifier derived from your private key. Share it to receive crypto — it cannot be used to move your funds.
- [What is a Crypto Wallet](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/crypto-wallet): A crypto wallet stores your private keys and lets you send, receive, and manage digital assets. Your coins live on the blockchain — the wallet controls access.
- [What is a Non-Custodial Wallet](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/non-custodial-wallet): A non-custodial wallet gives you full ownership of your private keys. No company can access, freeze, or lose your funds — you are your own bank.
- [What is a Cold Wallet](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/cold-wallet): A cold wallet stores private keys completely offline, making it immune to remote hacks. The most secure method for long-term crypto storage.
- [What is a Hot Wallet](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/hot-wallet): A hot wallet stays connected to the internet for fast, convenient access. Ideal for everyday transactions, DeFi, and dApp interactions.
- [What is a Crypto Wallet](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/crypto-wallet): A crypto wallet is an app or device that stores and manages the private keys controlling your cryptocurrency — the coins themselves live on the blockchain, and the wallet is what proves ownership and signs transactions.
- [What is a Wallet Address](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/wallet-address): A wallet address is a unique string of letters and numbers that identifies a destination on a blockchain — like an account number for crypto — derived from your public key so anyone can send funds to it without seeing your private key.
- [What is a Hardware Wallet](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/hardware-wallet): A hardware wallet is a physical device that stores your crypto private keys offline, keeping them isolated from internet-connected devices to protect against hacks, malware, and theft.
- [What is a Recovery Phrase](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/recovery-phrase): A recovery phrase is a list of 12–24 words that backs up your crypto wallet; anyone with it can restore the wallet and access the funds, so it must be kept secret and offline. It is also known as a seed phrase or mnemonic.
- [What is a Mnemonic](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/mnemonic): A mnemonic (mnemonic phrase) is the human-readable list of words — usually 12 or 24 — that represents a wallet's master key under the BIP-39 standard. It's the technical term for a seed phrase or recovery phrase.
- [What is a Custodial Wallet](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/custodial-wallet): A custodial wallet is one where a third party — such as an exchange — holds your private keys and controls your crypto on your behalf, meaning you rely on them to access your funds. It is the opposite of a non-custodial (self-custody) wallet.
- [What is a Paper Wallet](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/paper-wallet): A paper wallet is an offline method of storing crypto by printing or writing your public address and private key (often as QR codes) on paper, keeping the keys completely off any internet-connected device.
- [What is Multisig](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/multisig): Multisig (multi-signature) is a security setup that requires more than one private key to authorize a crypto transaction — for example, 2 of 3 keys — reducing the risk of theft or a single point of failure.
- [What is 2FA (Two-Factor Authentication)](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/2fa): 2FA (two-factor authentication) is a security method that requires two separate forms of verification to access an account — typically a password plus a one-time code — adding a second layer of protection beyond a password alone.
- [What is KYC (Know Your Customer)](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/kyc): KYC (Know Your Customer) is the identity-verification process that regulated financial services and centralized crypto exchanges use to confirm who their users are — typically requiring ID and personal details before you can trade or withdraw.
- [What is a Smart Account](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/smart-account): A smart account is a crypto wallet account controlled by programmable smart-contract code instead of a single private key alone, enabling features like transaction batching, spending limits, sponsored gas, and custom recovery.
- [What is a Passkey Wallet](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/passkey-wallet): A passkey wallet is a crypto wallet secured by a passkey — the device-based biometric credential used for passwordless logins — instead of, or alongside, a written seed phrase, trading transcription risk for platform dependence.
- [What is an MPC Wallet](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/mpc-wallet): An MPC wallet uses multi-party computation to split a private key into shares held on different devices or by different parties, so transactions are signed jointly and the complete key never exists in any single place.
- [What is Social Recovery](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/social-recovery): Social recovery is a wallet-recovery method where trusted parties called guardians can collectively restore your access if you lose your key — no single guardian can move funds, only an approved quorum can rotate the account's key.
- [What is Account Abstraction](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/account-abstraction): Account abstraction (notably via ERC-4337) lets crypto wallets behave like programmable smart contracts rather than basic key-based accounts — enabling features like gas paid in any token, social recovery, and batched transactions for a smoother user experience.
- [What are Gasless Transactions](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/gasless-transactions): Gasless transactions let users interact with a blockchain without paying the network fee from their own balance — a third party, usually a paymaster contract, sponsors the fee or accepts payment in a different token.
- [What is an AI Agent Wallet](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/ai-agent-wallet): An AI agent wallet is a crypto wallet operated by an autonomous AI agent, letting software hold funds and pay for services on its owner's behalf — ideally within strict, programmable limits the owner sets in advance.

### Scams & Threats

- [What is Phishing](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/phishing): Phishing is a scam in which attackers impersonate trusted entities — through fake websites, emails, or messages — to trick you into revealing sensitive information like your recovery phrase or private keys, or into signing malicious transactions.
- [What is a Rug Pull](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/rug-pull): A rug pull is a crypto scam in which developers attract investment into a project or token, then suddenly abandon it and drain the funds — leaving investors with worthless assets. The name comes from "pulling the rug out."
- [What is a Honeypot in Crypto](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/honeypot): A honeypot is a crypto scam token whose smart contract lets you buy in but secretly prevents you from selling, trapping your funds. The term also describes decoy systems used in cybersecurity to lure attackers.
- [What is a Dust Attack](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/dust-attack): A dust attack is a privacy attack in which tiny amounts of crypto ("dust") are sent to many wallet addresses, so the attacker can track and analyze transactions to try to deanonymize the owners.
- [What is Address Poisoning](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/address-poisoning): Address poisoning is a scam where attackers send tiny transactions from an address that looks almost identical to one you use, hoping you'll later copy the fake address from your transaction history and send funds to it.
- [What is a Crypto Drainer](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/crypto-drainer): A crypto drainer is malicious code — usually behind a fake website or app — designed to empty a wallet by tricking the owner into signing transactions or approvals that hand over control of their assets.
- [What is Pig Butchering](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/pig-butchering): Pig butchering is a long-con investment scam where fraudsters build trust over weeks — often through romance or friendly chat — then lure victims into fake crypto trading platforms and disappear with their deposits.
- [What is a SIM Swap](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/sim-swap): A SIM swap is an attack where a criminal takes over your phone number by tricking your mobile carrier, then uses it to intercept SMS codes and reset passwords — often to break into exchange accounts and email.
- [What is Blind Signing](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/blind-signing): Blind signing is approving a crypto transaction you can't fully read or verify — the wallet shows raw data instead of a human-readable action, so you can't be sure what you're authorizing. Clear signing is the safer opposite.
- [What is Clipboard Hijacking](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/clipboard-hijacking): Clipboard hijacking is malware that watches your device's clipboard for copied crypto addresses and silently swaps them for an attacker's address, so your funds go to a thief when you paste and send.
- [What is a Pump and Dump](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/pump-and-dump): A pump and dump is a scheme where insiders quietly accumulate a token, inflate its price with coordinated hype, then sell their holdings onto latecomers — leaving the buyers who believed the promotion holding a collapsing asset.
- [What is a Sybil Attack](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/sybil-attack): A Sybil attack floods a network with many fake identities controlled by one attacker to gain outsized influence — the reason blockchains tie voting power to something costly, like computing work or staked capital, instead of identities.
- [What is a 51% Attack](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/51-percent-attack): A 51% attack happens when one party controls the majority of a blockchain's mining power or stake, letting it reorganize recent blocks and double-spend its own transactions — though it cannot steal other users' funds or rewrite the rules.

### DeFi & Staking

- [What is DeFi](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/defi): Decentralized Finance (DeFi) refers to financial services — lending, borrowing, trading — built on blockchains without banks.
- [What is a dApp](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/dapp): A dApp (decentralized application) runs on a blockchain via smart contracts instead of a central server — giving users direct control without a company in the middle.
- [What is a DEX](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/dex): A DEX (decentralized exchange) lets you trade crypto directly from your wallet using smart contracts — no company holds your funds or requires an account.
- [What is an AMM](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/amm): An Automated Market Maker (AMM) is a type of DEX where liquidity pools and pricing formulas (like x*y=k) replace traditional order books. Uniswap, PancakeSwap, and Curve are AMMs.
- [What is a DAO](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/dao): A DAO (Decentralized Autonomous Organization) is an on-chain organization governed by token holders through proposals and votes, with rules enforced by smart contracts.
- [What is a Governance Token](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/governance-token): A governance token grants holders the right to vote on protocol decisions in a DAO. Examples include UNI (Uniswap), AAVE, COMP, and MKR (MakerDAO).
- [What is Yield Farming](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/yield-farming): Yield farming is the practice of providing liquidity, lending, or staking crypto assets across DeFi protocols to earn returns — typically in the form of token rewards or trading fees.
- [What is Impermanent Loss](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/impermanent-loss): Impermanent loss is the temporary loss liquidity providers experience when the price ratio of their pooled tokens changes versus simply holding them. It becomes permanent only when liquidity is withdrawn at the changed ratio.
- [What is Slippage](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/slippage): Slippage is the difference between the expected price of a trade and the actual executed price, caused by market movement or low liquidity between order placement and confirmation.
- [What is TVL](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/tvl): Total Value Locked (TVL) measures the total value of crypto assets deposited in a DeFi protocol. It is the standard metric for comparing the size and adoption of DeFi platforms.
- [What is Staking](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/staking): Staking means locking crypto to support a blockchain's proof-of-stake consensus and earn rewards in return.
- [What is Liquid Staking](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/liquid-staking): Liquid staking lets you stake crypto to earn rewards while receiving a tradable token that represents your staked position — so your capital stays usable in DeFi instead of being locked up.
- [What is Restaking](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/restaking): Restaking reuses assets that are already staked to help secure additional protocols and services, earning extra rewards on top of base staking yield — at the cost of extra slashing conditions and smart-contract risk.
- [What is MEV (Maximal Extractable Value)](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/mev): MEV (Maximal Extractable Value) is the extra profit that block producers or sophisticated traders can capture by strategically reordering, including, or excluding transactions within a block — for example through front-running or arbitrage.
- [What is Minting](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/mint): Minting is the process of creating a new token or NFT and recording it on the blockchain. For NFTs, it's how a digital item is first published on-chain; for tokens, it's how new units are brought into existence.
- [What is a Token Approval (Approve)](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/approve): Approving (token approval) is granting a smart contract or dApp permission to spend a specific token from your wallet. It's required to interact with DeFi and DEXs, but over-broad approvals can be a security risk.
- [What is Revoking a Token Approval](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/revoke): Revoking is removing a smart contract's previously granted permission to spend tokens from your wallet. It's a key security practice to close off access from dApps you no longer use or no longer trust.
- [What is a Bonding Curve](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/bonding-curve): A bonding curve is a smart-contract pricing mechanism that sets a token's price algorithmically from its supply: buying mints tokens and moves the price up the curve, selling returns them and moves it down.
- [What is Collateral](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/collateral): Collateral is an asset pledged to secure a loan or leveraged position — in DeFi, borrowers typically lock crypto worth more than they borrow, and the protocol automatically liquidates it if its value falls too far.

### Trading & Markets

- [What is Market Cap](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/market-cap): Market capitalization is the total value of a cryptocurrency calculated by multiplying its current price by its circulating supply. Used to rank assets by relative size.
- [What is ATH](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/ath): All-Time High (ATH) is the highest price a cryptocurrency has ever reached on a major exchange. Often referenced as a psychological and technical benchmark by traders.
- [What is a Bull Market](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/bull-market): A bull market is a sustained period of rising prices and positive sentiment in crypto markets — typically lasting months and characterized by new ATHs across major assets.
- [What is a Bear Market](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/bear-market): A bear market is a sustained period of falling prices and pessimistic sentiment — typically a drawdown of 20% or more from recent highs lasting weeks to many months.
- [What is HODL](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/hodl): HODL is a long-term holding strategy popular in crypto, originating from a misspelling of "hold" on a 2013 Bitcoin forum post. HODLers buy and hold through volatility rather than trading.
- [What is a Whale](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/whale): A whale is an individual or entity that holds a very large amount of a cryptocurrency — large enough that their buy or sell decisions can move the market price.
- [What is a Limit Order](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/limit-order): A limit order is a trade type that executes only when the market reaches a specified target price. Trust Wallet supports limit orders for swaps directly in the app.
- [What is a Market Order](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/market-order): A market order is a trade that executes immediately at the best available current price, prioritizing speed over price control.
- [What is a Stop Loss Order](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/stop-loss-order): A stop loss order automatically closes a position when the price drops to a specified level, capping potential losses on a leveraged or spot trade.
- [What is a Take Profit Order](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/take-profit-order): A take profit order automatically closes a position when the price rises to a specified level, locking in gains without requiring constant monitoring.
- [What is a Swap](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/swap): A swap is the act of exchanging one cryptocurrency directly for another, often within a wallet or decentralized exchange, without needing to convert to fiat or use a traditional order book.
- [What is a Memecoin](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/memecoin): A memecoin is a cryptocurrency whose value comes from community, culture, and speculation rather than underlying utility — its price runs on attention, rallying when the crowd arrives and collapsing when it moves on.
- [What are Tokenized Stocks](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/tokenized-stocks): Tokenized stocks are blockchain tokens designed to track the price of publicly traded shares, giving crypto users stock price exposure on-chain — usually without the legal rights that come with direct share ownership.
- [What is Liquidity](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/liquidity): Liquidity is how easily an asset can be bought or sold without moving its price — a liquid market absorbs large trades with little impact, while an illiquid one swings sharply on even small orders.
- [What is an Order Book](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/order-book): An order book is an exchange's live list of buy and sell orders for an asset, organized by price — trades happen when a bid and an ask meet, and the book's depth shows how much buying and selling interest sits at each level.
- [What is the Bid-Ask Spread](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/bid-ask-spread): The bid-ask spread is the gap between the highest price buyers will pay for an asset and the lowest price sellers will accept — a built-in cost of every trade and one of the clearest signals of how liquid a market is.
- [What is Volatility](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/volatility): Volatility measures how much and how fast an asset's price moves — crypto markets are known for high volatility, which creates both the outsized gains that attract traders and the sharp drawdowns that punish overexposure.
- [What is Vesting](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/vesting): Vesting is the scheduled release of tokens over time — instead of receiving their full allocation at launch, teams and early investors unlock tokens gradually, aligning their incentives with the project's long-term success.
- [What is the Flippening](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/flippening): The flippening is a hypothetical event in which Ethereum's total market value overtakes Bitcoin's — a milestone watched as a barometer of whether the market values programmable smart-contract platforms above digital gold.

### Non-Fungible Tokens

- [What is an NFT](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/nft): A Non-Fungible Token (NFT) is a unique digital asset recorded on a blockchain, used for art, collectibles, and gaming.
- [What is an NFT Floor Price](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/nft-floor-price): An NFT floor price is the lowest listed price for any item in an NFT collection — a quick gauge of the cheapest way in and the collection's momentum, though it reflects asking prices, not what buyers actually pay.

